# The MJML-generated email templates are byte-compared in CI (ci.yml `frontend`
# job runs tools/email/verify-regeneration.sh). A CRLF checkout would make that
# diff fail on Windows/autocrlf clones for a reason that has nothing to do with
# the templates, so mark them binary-as-far-as-line-endings-go: `-text` turns off
# eol conversion in BOTH directions, so what is in the index is what lands in the
# working tree. (`-text` alone does it; `eol=` has no effect once `text` is unset,
# so it is not spelled here.)
backend/crates/netroll-adapters/templates/*.html -text
backend/crates/netroll-app/templates/*.html      -text
tools/email/src/*.mjml                           -text
tools/email/palette.lock.json                    -text

# The comparison itself has the same dependency. A CRLF checkout of the script
# gives `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r`, which does not execute — the local half of the
# regeneration check would be unrunnable on exactly the platform the block
# above exists for. And tokens.css is a byte-exact INPUT: build.mjs slices its
# dark block, so a stray `\r` inside a value would be substituted into every
# generated template.
tools/email/verify-regeneration.sh                -text
tools/email/build.mjs                             -text
frontend/src/ui/tokens/tokens.css                 -text
